\f0\fs40 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Multi-model orchestration in Amazon Bedrock Converse API\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf3 The AI industry has a continuous pipeline of models in various stages of development and release. As one frontier model gains adoption, another is already approaching launch with new capabilities, different tradeoffs, and fresh evaluation benchmarks.\
This creates a challenge for enterprise teams. The problem is not deciding whether a new model is better than the last one. The problem is building applications so tightly coupled to today\'92s model that adopting tomorrow\'92s requires a rewrite.\
Organizations that realize the most value from AI will not necessarily be the ones that pick the right model first. They will be the ones that build reusable architectures that can accommodate changes in models, requirements, and business priorities without repeated redesign efforts.\
The Amazon Bedrock Converse API addresses this directly. Rather than building application logic around a single model, developers can use a consistent interface across multiple foundation models, allowing routing, observability, and governance patterns to remain stable as models change.\
In this post, I will use Claude Opus 4.8 as the implementation example to demonstrate how to build a multi-model architecture on AWS that reduces lock-in, simplifies model evaluation, supports intelligent model routing, and turns future model upgrades into a configuration change instead of an engineering project.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Why multi-model architecture matters on AWS\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf3 Most teams do not realize they are creating model lock-in until it becomes expensive.\
The first implementation works. The proof of concept succeeds. Then a new model appears with better reasoning, lower latency, or a more attractive pricing structure. Suddenly application code, prompt formatting, SDK integrations, and evaluation tooling all need updates.\
The most common challenges include:\
\pard\tx360\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa36\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1 \cf3 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Model coupling through provider-specific APIs\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Evaluation lock-in that prevents meaningful A/B testing\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Operational risk when a model is deprecated or unavailable\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Fragmented observability across providers\
\pard\tx360\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa180\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1 \cf3 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Difficulty controlling cost and latency across workloads\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0
\cf3 The solution is not continuously switching models. The solution is introducing a routing and abstraction layer early.\
This is the same architectural principle that drove API gateways, container orchestration, and cloud-native platforms. Decouple the implementation from the interface.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 What the Bedrock Converse API solves\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf3 The Amazon Bedrock Converse API provides a standardized interface for interacting with multiple foundation models through a single request format.\
Instead of writing separate integrations for Claude, Llama, Mistral, and future models, developers can use a common API structure that standardizes:\
\pard\tx360\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa36\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1 \cf3 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Request payloads\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Conversation history\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Inference configuration\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Tool use patterns\
\pard\tx360\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa180\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1 \cf3 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Response structures\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0
\cf3 One implementation pattern for a multi-model architecture on AWS is shown below:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Client
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22   
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  API Gateway
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22   
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  Lambda or FastAPI service
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22   
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  Amazon Bedrock Converse API
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22   
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  Claude Opus 4.8 | fallback model | task-specific model
\f0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0
\cf3 The architectural advantage is straightforward:\
Your application code does not change when your model selection changes.\
The model identifier changes. The surrounding orchestration does not.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Prerequisites\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf3 To follow the examples in this post, you need:\
\pard\tx360\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa36\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1 \cf3 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone An AWS account with access to Amazon Bedrock\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Model access enabled for the models you want to test\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Python 3.10 or later\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Boto3 installed locally\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone AWS credentials configured for your environment\
\pard\tx360\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa180\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1 \cf3 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone IAM permissions to call Amazon Bedrock Runtime APIs\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0
\cf3 Install the required Python packages:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 pip install boto3 botocore
\f0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0
\cf3 Set the AWS Region and model identifiers as environment variables:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf4 export\cf3  \cf5 AWS_REGION\cf6 =\cf7 "us-east-1"
\f0\fs24 \cf3 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22 \cf4 export\cf3  \cf5 PRIMARY_MODEL_ID\cf6 =\cf7 "us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8"
\f0\fs24 \cf3 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22 \cf4 export\cf3  \cf5 FALLBACK_MODEL_ID\cf6 =\cf7 "your-fallback-model-id"
\f0\fs24 \cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0
\cf3 Use the model ID or inference profile ID that is enabled in your AWS account and supported in your selected Region.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Using Claude Opus 4.8 as the primary model\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf3 For this walkthrough, Claude Opus 4.8 is used as the primary model for complex reasoning workloads.\
The model selection is not the main architectural decision. The important decision is where the model selection happens. In this pattern, application teams do not call a specific model directly. They call a routing layer that selects a model based on workload type, cost profile, latency requirements, or fallback behavior.\
That makes Claude Opus 4.8 a concrete implementation example, not a hard dependency in the application architecture.\
This pattern applies to workloads such as:\
\pard\tx360\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa36\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1 \cf3 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Multi-stage autonomous agent workflows\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Earnings call analysis\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Regulatory document review\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Threat intelligence investigations\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Literature reviews in life sciences\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Enterprise knowledge management systems\
\pard\tx360\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa180\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1 \cf3 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Code analysis and technical planning\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0
\cf3 The value comes from the architecture: you can evaluate and replace the model without redesigning the surrounding platform.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Amazon Bedrock Converse API example\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf3 The Converse API standardizes interactions across models, making implementation easier to reuse across workloads.\
The following helper creates a Bedrock Runtime client and reads model settings from environment variables.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f4\b\fs22 \cf4 import
\f2\b0 \cf3  os
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 \u8232 
\f4\b\fs22 \cf4 import
\f2\b0 \cf3  boto3
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f4\b\fs22 \cf4 from
\f2\b0 \cf3  botocore.exceptions 
\f4\b \cf4 import
\f2\b0 \cf3  ClientError
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 \u8232 
\f2\fs22 AWS_REGION \cf6 =\cf3  os.getenv(\cf7 "AWS_REGION"\cf3 , \cf7 "us-east-1"\cf3 )
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22 PRIMARY_MODEL_ID \cf6 =\cf3  os.environ[\cf7 "PRIMARY_MODEL_ID"\cf3 ]
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22 FALLBACK_MODEL_ID \cf6 =\cf3  os.environ[\cf7 "FALLBACK_MODEL_ID"\cf3 ]
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 \u8232 
\f2\fs22 bedrock \cf6 =\cf3  boto3.client(\cf7 "bedrock-runtime"\cf3 , region_name\cf6 =\cf3 AWS_REGION)
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 \u8232 \u8232 
\f4\b\fs22 \cf8 def
\f2\b0 \cf3  extract_text(response: \cf4 dict\cf3 ) \cf6 ->\cf3  \cf4 str\cf3 :
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22     
\f5\i \cf9 """Extract the first text response from a Converse API response."""
\f0\i0\fs24 \cf3 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22     
\f4\b \cf8 return
\f2\b0 \cf3  response[\cf7 "output"\cf3 ][\cf7 "message"\cf3 ][\cf7 "content"\cf3 ][\cf10 0\cf3 ][\cf7 "text"\cf3 ]
\f0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Single-turn inference\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf3 A basic Converse API request uses a consistent message structure regardless of the underlying model.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 response \cf6 =\cf3  bedrock.converse(
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22     modelId\cf6 =\cf3 PRIMARY_MODEL_ID,
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22     messages\cf6 =\cf3 [
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22         \{
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22             \cf7 "role"\cf3 : \cf7 "user"\cf3 ,
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22             \cf7 "content"\cf3 : [
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22                 \{
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22                     \cf7 "text"\cf3 : \cf7 "What are the key tradeoffs between synchronous and asynchronous Lambda invocation?"
\f0\fs24 \cf3 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22                 \}
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22             ],
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22         \}
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22     ],
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22     inferenceConfig\cf6 =\cf3 \{
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22         \cf7 "maxTokens"\cf3 : \cf10 1024\cf3 ,
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22         \cf7 "temperature"\cf3 : \cf10 0.3\cf3 ,
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22     \},
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22 )
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 \u8232 
\f2\fs22 \cf4 print\cf3 (extract_text(response))
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22 \cf4 print\cf3 (
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22     \cf11 f"Tokens \'97 input: \cf7 \{\cf3 response[\cf7 'usage'\cf3 ][\cf7 'inputTokens'\cf3 ]\cf7 \}\cf11 , "
\f0\fs24 \cf3 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22     \cf11 f"output: \cf7 \{\cf3 response[\cf7 'usage'\cf3 ][\cf7 'outputTokens'\cf3 ]\cf7 \}\cf11 "
\f0\fs24 \cf3 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22 )
\f0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0
\cf3 This example sends one user message to the primary model and prints both the generated response and token usage. Token usage is useful for cost tracking, monitoring, and evaluation pipelines.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Multi-turn conversations\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf3 Conversation state is represented as a message array. To continue a conversation, append the previous user and assistant messages and pass the full array back to the Converse API.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 conversation \cf6 =\cf3  [
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22     \{
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22         \cf7 "role"\cf3 : \cf7 "user"\cf3 ,
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22         \cf7 "content"\cf3 : [
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22             \{
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22                 \cf7 "text"\cf3 : \cf7 "I am designing a RAG pipeline on AWS. Where should I start?"
\f0\fs24 \cf3 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22             \}
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22         ],
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22     \},
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22     \{
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22         \cf7 "role"\cf3 : \cf7 "assistant"\cf3 ,
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22         \cf7 "content"\cf3 : [
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22             \{
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22                 \cf7 "text"\cf3 : \cf7 "Start with your retrieval strategy. Are you using structured or unstructured data?"
\f0\fs24 \cf3 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22             \}
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22         ],
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22     \},
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22     \{
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22         \cf7 "role"\cf3 : \cf7 "user"\cf3 ,
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22         \cf7 "content"\cf3 : [
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22             \{
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22                 \cf7 "text"\cf3 : \cf7 "Unstructured data, mainly PDFs and internal wikis."
\f0\fs24 \cf3 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22             \}
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22         ],
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22     \},
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22 ]
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 \u8232 
\f2\fs22 response \cf6 =\cf3  bedrock.converse(
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22     modelId\cf6 =\cf3 PRIMARY_MODEL_ID,
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22     messages\cf6 =\cf3 conversation,
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22     system\cf6 =\cf3 [
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22         \{
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22             \cf7 "text"\cf3 : \cf7 "You are an AWS solutions architect. Provide concise, practical guidance."
\f0\fs24 \cf3 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22         \}
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22     ],
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22     inferenceConfig\cf6 =\cf3 \{
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22         \cf7 "maxTokens"\cf3 : \cf10 2048\cf3 ,
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22         \cf7 "temperature"\cf3 : \cf10 0.4\cf3 ,
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22     \},
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\fs22 )
\f0\fs24 \uc0\u8232 \u8232 
\f2\fs22 \cf4 print\cf3 (extract_text(response))
\f0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0
\cf3 The same message structure can be reused with another supported model by changing the model ID. That is the core benefit of keeping model selection outside the application workflow.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Model routing in Amazon Bedrock\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf3 Model routing is one of the most practical patterns you can implement with the Converse API.\
Instead of exposing model identifiers to application teams, classify requests by workload type:\
\pard\tx360\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa36\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1 \cf3 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Reasoning\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Coding\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Analysis\
\ls5\ilvl0
